# PyGit: Git Internals Rebuild & DAG Visualizer

A reimplementation of the core Git architecture from scratch in Python, complete with a Flask API and an interactive React Flow frontend. This project explores the fundamentals of version control by rebuilding low-level object storage (blobs, trees, commits), SHA-1 hashing, and zlib compression directly matching native Git behavior[cite: 1].

## Architectural Overview

* **Content-Addressable Storage Engine (Python)**: Objects are hashed via SHA-1, compressed using `zlib`, and stored in `.pygit/objects/`[cite: 1].
* **The Object Model**: Implements `blob` (raw file data), `tree` (directory pointers), and `commit` (snapshots forming the DAG)[cite: 1].
* **Staging Area (Index)**: A JSON-backed index tracks file paths and their associated blob hashes prior to tree construction[cite: 1].
* **REST API (Flask)**: Serves the internal `.pygit` DAG structure as JSON endpoints.
* **Frontend Visualizer (React & TypeScript)**: Consumes the API to render interactive, draggable commit nodes and animated branch edges using React Flow.

## Getting Started

### Prerequisites
* Python 3.6+
* Node.js & npm

### 1. Engine Setup
```bash
git clone [https://github.com/MustafaMaj23/pygit.git](https://github.com/MustafaMaj23/pygit.git)
cd pygit
chmod +x pygit.py

# Initialize a repo and make a commit
./pygit.py init
echo "Hello World" > file.txt
./pygit.py add file.txt
./pygit.py commit "Initial commit"